f3ec809b-5d1d-4c2d-a679-0114125cef1d	BUS 2220	Digital Marketing Strategy	1619ce49-264f-4256-97fe-0c830d54f689	This course covers how to market a brand via the 7 main digital marketing platforms. Topics include understanding the customer experience, selecting and building a digital platform, creating a content strategy, digital marketing vehicles, and assessing performance. Assignments include readings and individual assignments, as well as group work where teams develop and present digital marketing strategies. Students will also earn marketing certifications in HubSpot as part of this course. Prerequisite: BUS 1600. [1.5]	2	\N	0	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-20 05:56:36.909424+00	2026-02-20 05:56:36.909424+00
e04b730f-1482-499d-b53f-62af2be5c4b7	BUS 2310	Introduction to Entrepreneurship	1619ce49-264f-4256-97fe-0c830d54f689	This course addresses the unique entrepreneurial experience of conceiving, evaluating, creating, managing, and potentially selling a business. The goal is to provide a comprehensive background with practical application of important concepts applicable to entrepreneurial environment. In addition to creative aspects, key business areas of finance, accounting, marketing and management will be addressed from an entrepreneurial perspective. The course relies on classroom discussion, participation, guest speakers, and case analysis. Students will need to draw upon their business education and experience, and apply it to the task of launching a new venture. Students are expected to interact with the business community and actively participate in classroom discussions and exercises. [1.5]	2	\N	0	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-20 05:56:36.91122+00	2026-02-20 05:56:36.91122+00
c6b92f56-2e17-4718-adfa-92c49d0e5ea2	BUS 2320	Innovation Strategy	1619ce49-264f-4256-97fe-0c830d54f689	What makes some organizations more innovative than others? In this course, students will learn how to improve an organization's innovation capabilities. Major themes include how to foster innovation within the firm, how to commercialize innovative ideas through new products and services, how to protect innovation from competitors, and how to overcome common barriers to innovation. Students will also improve their own innovation abilities through a project in which they pitch a new product or service. Note that while we will discuss examples of technical innovation, this is not a tech course; rather, this course is about how to create an effective strategy for innovation in a wide range of organizations. [1.5]	2	\N	0	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-20 05:56:36.913071+00	2026-02-20 05:56:36.913071+00
71a4167d-ecb2-474f-99f0-d780a0c33222	BUS 2630	Sports Analytics	1619ce49-264f-4256-97fe-0c830d54f689	Sports Analytics uses data and quantitative methods to measure performance and make decisions to gain advantage in the competitive sports arena. Pre-requisite courses have exposed students to powerful quantitative methods such as linear regression and constrained optimization.  These quantitative methods can give operations a competitive advantage.  In this course, students gain further experience in applying quantitative methods to problems in the context of sports.  Typical questions in sports analytics include: How to predict future performance of players or teams?  How to rank players or teams?  Which decision is more likely to lead to a win?  We will cover several sports, but to be able to 'go deep' we will focus significantly on baseball, football, and soccer.  In class, we will analyze real sports data with powerful quantitative methods.  As a result, the course will sharpen students' analytics skills.  These skills are equally useful in many areas of business. Pre-requisites: One of BUS 1700, ECON 3012, or instructor consent, AND One of ECON 1500, ECON 1510, MATH 1011, MATH 2810, MATH 2820, PSY 2100, PSY-PC 2110, or SOC 2100. [1.5]	2	\N	0	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-20 05:56:36.914909+00	2026-02-20 05:56:36.914909+00
